## Reached at
  A CherryPy HTTP service on `http://localhost:8080`, driven exclusively through
  HTTP GET requests to the `/api` route (the same app is also mounted under the
  configured `url_base` prefix, but callers use the root mount):
      http://localhost:8080/api?mode=<mode>&apikey=<key>&<mode-specific params>
  The serving daemon is the project'"'"'s own Python program, started by the image'"'"'s
  entrypoint inside a restart loop as:
      python3 /app/SABnzbd.py -s 0.0.0.0:8080 -b 0 -f /config
  The project source tree is `/app` (package `sabnzbd`, entry point `/app/SABnzbd.py`,
  runtime Python 3.10); its runtime configuration directory is `/config`, holding
  `sabnzbd.ini`. Callers additionally scan `/proc/<pid>/cmdline` for the literal
  string `SABnzbd.py` and require two answers three seconds apart to come from the
  same such process, so the answering service must be this daemon, not a stub, a
  system tool, or a proxy. Callers also insist their own HTTP client is the
  interpreter'"'"'s standard-library `urllib.request`, so the service must be a genuine
  HTTP server reachable over TCP on port 8080.

  The notification-script feature the callers exercise is reached through two chains,
  both landing in the same project-owned script runner in `sabnzbd/notifier.py`:
  - `mode=test_nscript` dispatches through the `_api_table` in `sabnzbd/api.py`
    (`"test_nscript": (_api_test_nscript, 3)`); the handler passes the request'"'"'s
    query parameters through as the `test` mapping to the runner, which takes the
    script name and the parameters from that mapping.
  - Real notification events reach the runner through `send_notification` in
    `sabnzbd/notifier.py`, which reads the script name and parameters from the
    `[nscript]` configuration values instead; the callers fire pause/resume events
    via `mode=pause` and `mode=resume`, which flow through the downloader into this
    notifier. The script name is resolved against the configured `script_dir`
    (`make_script_path` in `sabnzbd/filesystem.py`) and the resolved file is executed
    through `build_and_run_command` in `sabnzbd/misc.py`.

## Must provide
  HTTP GET route `/api` on port 8080, query-parameter driven, accepting `apikey` on
  every request, with at least these `mode` values:
      version          (no key required by the dispatcher)
      queue            (accepts output=json)
      get_config       (accepts section, output)
      set_config       (accepts section, keyword, value, output)
      test_nscript     (accepts nscript_script, nscript_parameters, output)
      pause
      resume
  Configuration surface, readable and writable through get_config/set_config and
  persisted in `/config/sabnzbd.ini`:
      [misc]    api_key          string; callers read this file line by line and use
                                 any line starting with `api_key` as the request key
      [misc]    script_dir       string; directory in which notification script names
                                 are resolved
      [nscript] nscript_enable
      [nscript] nscript_script
      [nscript] nscript_parameters
      [nscript] nscript_prio_pause_resume
  Notification-script execution, owned by the project and reachable from both chains:
  executing the executable named by `nscript_script` (a bare filename resolved inside
  `script_dir`), invoked both by the `test_nscript` request path (parameters taken
  from that request) and by pause/resume event notifications (parameters taken from
  the `[nscript]` configuration), and delivering its event data to the executed
  process. A nonexistent script name passed to `test_nscript` must be reported through
  the API rather than crashing the request.
  A daemon process whose command line contains `SABnzbd.py` and stays stable while the
  callers poll it.

## Called as
  Every contact is one HTTP GET against `http://localhost:8080/api` with URL-encoded
  query parameters; every value below is passed as a query string. The `apikey`
  parameter is a string; callers take it from the `api_key` line of
  `/config/sabnzbd.ini`.
      {"mode": "get_config", "section": "nscript", "output": "json"}
      {"mode": "get_config", "section": "misc", "output": "json"}
      {"mode": "set_config", "section": "nscript", "keyword": <one of the nscript
          keys above>, "value": <a string>, "output": "json"}
      {"mode": "set_config", "section": "misc", "keyword": "script_dir",
          "value": <a string>, "output": "json"}
      {"mode": "test_nscript", "nscript_script": <a string>,
          "nscript_parameters": <a string>, "output": "json"}
      {"mode": "test_nscript", "nscript_script": <a string>,
          "nscript_parameters": ""}
      {"mode": "version"}
      {"mode": "queue", "output": "json"}
      {"mode": "pause"}
      {"mode": "resume"}
  `nscript_script` is a string naming a file inside `script_dir`; callers pass both
  the name of a script they themselves staged there and names of files that do not
  exist. `nscript_parameters` is a single string; callers pass empty strings as well
  as multi-word strings containing spaces. `value` on set_config is a string. The
  event chain is driven by issuing the pause/resume modes repeatedly with short
  delays between them while the `[nscript]` settings name a staged script.

## Setup required
  The daemon must already be running and answering on 0.0.0.0:8080 before any call
  lands: the image entrypoint launches it in a restart loop with `-f /config`, and
  callers poll `get_config` for up to two minutes for a ready, stable process.
  `/config/sabnzbd.ini` must exist and carry an `api_key` entry; every mode except
  `version` is matched against it.
  Callers and the daemon share one filesystem: callers create `script_dir` when
  absent, copy their own executable script into it and mark it executable (0755),
  and later read files under `/tmp` written during that script'"'"'s execution and scan
  `/proc` for the daemon. Notification events only reach the script runner when
  `nscript_enable` is set and the corresponding per-event priority flag
  (`nscript_prio_pause_resume` for pause/resume) is set; callers configure both
  through the API before firing events.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side is a pytest process that performs all of the following itself;
  none of it is part of the implementation and none of it must be rebuilt:
  - It sends every request with the Python standard library'"'"'s HTTP client and checks
    that this client module resolves inside the running interpreter'"'"'s own tree.
  - It stages a caller-owned bash script into the configured `script_dir` under a
    fixed name of its own choosing; that script records its own argument vector and
    environment into a file under `/tmp` when it is executed, and the caller polls
    those records (and other caller-created paths under `/tmp`) to observe what the
    executed process received.
  - It reads `/proc/<pid>/cmdline` entries to identify and track the daemon process
    by the string `SABnzbd.py`.
  - It reads `/config/sabnzbd.ini` directly to discover a working API key.
