  substitute module resolved elsewhere. The package is installed by npm during
  the image build (declared dependency set: sequelize 3.30.0, pg ^4.4.6,
  pg-hstore ^2.3.1).

## Must provide
  Sequelize                constructor; the package'"'"'s module export; called with
                           four arguments (database, username, password,
                           options); carries a static DataTypes namespace
  DataTypes.TEXT           attribute type token used in model definitions
  DataTypes.JSONB          attribute type token used in model definitions
  sequelize.define         instance method; the caller binds its result as the
                           Model class on which the static methods below are
                           invoked
  sequelize.sync           instance method, async (callers await it)
  sequelize.authenticate   instance method, async (callers await it)
  sequelize.query          instance method, async (callers await it)
  sequelize.close          instance method, async (callers await it)
  Model.create             static method, async (callers await it)
  Model.bulkCreate         static method, async (callers await it)
  Model.findAll            static method, async (callers await it)
  Model.findOne            static method, async (callers await it)
  Model.count              static method, async (callers await it)
  Model.findAndCountAll    static method, async (callers await it)
  Model.update             static method, async (callers await it)
  Model.destroy            static method, async (callers await it)
  The dialect selected by options.dialect must operate against a live
  PostgreSQL server through the pg package installed alongside it.

## Called as
  new Sequelize(database, username, password, options)
      database, username, password — 1st to 3rd positional arguments; strings.
      options — 4th positional argument; an object with host (string), port
      (number), dialect (callers pass the string '"'"'postgres'"'"'), logging
      (boolean; callers pass false).

  sequelize.define(modelName, attributes, options)
      modelName — 1st positional argument; a string. attributes — 2nd
      positional argument; a plain object mapping column names to DataTypes
      tokens; callers define one TEXT column and one JSONB column. options —
      3rd positional argument; an object with tableName (a string chosen by
      the caller) and timestamps (boolean; callers pass false).

  Project.create(values) and Project.bulkCreate(rows)
      values — 1st positional argument; a plain object mapping column names to
      values — strings, numbers, or plain objects for the JSONB column. rows —
      1st positional argument; an array of such objects.

  Project.findAll(options), Project.findOne(options), Project.count(options),
  Project.findAndCountAll(options), Project.destroy(options)
      options — 1st positional argument; a plain object. options.where — a
      plain object mapping a column name to a condition object; for the JSONB
      column, the condition object'"'"'s own property names are the JSON path keys
      (any strings) and its values are strings, numbers, or nested plain
      objects, where a nested object extends the path by one more level.
      options.attributes — an array of column-name strings. options.raw — a
      boolean. count is called with only options.where.

  Project.update(values, options)
      values — 1st positional argument; a plain object mapping column names to
      new values. options — 2nd positional argument; an object with where. In
      the existing calls, this where clause addresses the plain TEXT column
      rather than the JSONB column.

  sequelize.sync(options)      options — an object with a boolean force flag.
  sequelize.query(sql)         sql — 1st positional argument; a string.
  sequelize.authenticate()     no arguments.
  sequelize.close()            no arguments.

## Setup required
  A PostgreSQL server reachable at DB_HOST:DB_PORT (defaults: host "postgres",
  port 5432) with database DB_NAME, user DB_USER, password DB_PASSWORD, all
  supplied via environment variables. The caller connects to the same server
  independently through a second client library to read table contents, so
  effects must flow through real SQL executed on that server.
  The Node binary at /usr/local/bin/node executes the calling scripts with
  working directory /app.
  The package must be the npm-installed tree at /app/node_modules/sequelize
  with pg available beside it. Tables are created and dropped by the caller
  via sequelize.sync, so the implementation owns no schema, no seed data, and
  no service startup.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side is a pytest process that builds self-contained Node scripts
  — each script requires the package, prints the resolved module path,
  constructs the Sequelize instance and a two-column model, runs an embedded
  async body, and closes the connection — and runs each script through
