This block states only where and how existing callers reach the implementation.
It does not state expected outcomes or prescribe implementation structure.

## Reached at
  Two project-owned artifacts, both built inside /sqlite from the SQLite source
  tree (tag version-3.30.1) that the image build clones there. Callers address
  both by absolute path only and run everything with working directory /sqlite.

  1. The CLI shell, run with a multi-statement SQL script on stdin:

         subprocess.run(["/sqlite/sqlite3", ":memory:"], input=sql_script,
                        capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30, cwd="/sqlite")

     The zipfile virtual table module is compiled into this shell binary: the
     shell source textually INCLUDEs ext/misc/zipfile.c at build time and the
     shell registers the module when it opens the database, so
     `CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE ... USING zipfile(...)` resolves inside the shell
     with no extension-loading step.

  2. The loadable module, loaded by absolute path into a SQLite session hosted
     by the system Python binding (which has no zipfile module built in, so the
     module code reached on this route is exactly the shared object):

         /usr/bin/python3 -c <inline script> <archive path>     (cwd=/sqlite)

         conn = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")
         conn.enable_load_extension(True)
         conn.load_extension("/sqlite/zipfile")

     sqlite3 load extension resolves the entry point from the file name.

  Both artifacts are produced by the image build in /sqlite:

      ./configure && make
      gcc -fPIC -shared -I. ext/misc/zipfile.c -L.libs -lsqlite3 -lz -o zipfile.so

  Callers refuse any stand-in: the run fails unless /sqlite/sqlite3 is a genuine
  ELF executable and /sqlite/zipfile.so a genuine ELF shared object.

## Must provide
  /sqlite/sqlite3        ELF executable CLI shell with the `zipfile` virtual
                         table module registered in-process
  /sqlite/zipfile.so     ELF shared object, loadable through sqlite3'"'"'s
                         load_extension mechanism
  sqlite3_zipfile_init   entry point exported by zipfile.so; standard
                         load-extension signature
                         int (sqlite3*, char**, const sqlite3_api_routines*)
  zipfile                the virtual-table module name registered by both
                         artifacts; created as
                         CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE <t> USING zipfile('"'"'<path>'"'"')
                         with one module argument, a quoted string literal
                         naming the archive file
  module columns, in declared order: name, mode, mtime, sz, rawdata, data,
  method — callers address name, mode, mtime, data by name, and positional
  INSERT ... SELECT * statements depend on this order

## Called as
  CLI route — SQL text fed on stdin to `/sqlite/sqlite3 :memory:`; statement
  forms callers issue against the zipfile table <t>:

    CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE <t> USING zipfile('"'"'<archive path>'"'"');
        module argument: one quoted SQL string literal naming the archive file;
        callers pass absolute paths under /tmp, a fresh unique name per run

    INSERT INTO <t>(name, mode, mtime, data) VALUES(<e1>, <e2>, <e3>, <e4>);
    REPLACE INTO <t>(name, mode, mtime, data) VALUES(<e1>, <e2>, <e3>, <e4>);
        name  — named column; a SQL expression (observed callers pass string
                literals, including the empty string)
        mode  — named column; a SQL expression (observed callers pass integer
                literals)
        mtime — named column; a SQL expression (observed callers pass integer
                literals)
        data  — named column; a SQL expression (observed callers pass string
                literals)

    INSERT INTO <t>(name, mode, mtime, data) SELECT <e1>, <e2>, <e3>, <e4>;
        the same named columns fed from a SELECT list

    UPDATE <t> SET name = <expr> WHERE <expr>;

    REPLACE INTO <t> SELECT * FROM <regular table>;
    INSERT INTO <t> SELECT * FROM <regular table>;
        positional writes: values map onto the module'"'"'s columns in declared
        order; source tables are created with more columns than the module has,
        and extra source columns are dropped before they reach the module

    SELECT quote(name), mode, mtime, hex(data) FROM <t> ...
    SELECT name FROM <t> WHERE name = <expr>;
    SELECT COUNT(*) FROM <t>;
        read-back queries over the same columns, with WHERE and ORDER BY

  Loadable-module route — the same statement forms issued as text through
  conn.execute(...) on the in-memory connection after
  conn.load_extension("/sqlite/zipfile"). The inline harness script takes one
  argument after -c: a string, the archive path it interpolates into the
  CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE statement.

## Setup required
  - Working directory /sqlite: callers set cwd="/sqlite" on every spawned
    process; the artifacts and the source tree they are rebuilt from live there.
  - LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/sqlite/.libs must hold in the environment (it is image
    ENV): /sqlite/sqlite3 links dynamically against /sqlite/.libs/libsqlite3.so,
    and zipfile.so links against the same library and against libz.
  - The shell route performs no extension loading: the `zipfile` module must
    already be registered inside /sqlite/sqlite3.
  - The module route depends on sqlite3'"'"'s load_extension ABI: entry point name
    derived from the file name (sqlite3_zipfile_init), called on a connection
    with enable_load_extension(True).
  - Lifecycle: src/shell.c.in INCLUDEs ext/misc/zipfile.c, so the module inside
    /sqlite/sqlite3 and the module inside /sqlite/zipfile.so are two builds of
    the same source. Callers exercise both builds independently and check each
    artifact at its exact absolute path; rebuilding only one leaves the other
    route running stale code.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side is a pytest process that reaches both artifacts through
  subprocess:
  - CLI route: the runner spawns /sqlite/sqlite3 with a generated SQL script on
    stdin and reads the process output.
  - Module route: the runner spawns the system interpreter /usr/bin/python3
    with an inline -c script that uses Python'"'"'s standard-library sqlite3
    binding to open an in-memory database, load /sqlite/zipfile, and issue the
    SQL. The interpreter and its binding are pre-existing system components.
  - The /tmp archive files are caller-created resources: the harness generates
    a unique name per run and removes the file afterwards.
  None of these — the pytest process, the runner helpers, the inline scripts,
  the interpreter and binding, the archive files — are part of the
  implementation. The implementation surface is exactly /sqlite/sqlite3,
  /sqlite/zipfile.so, and the source tree in /sqlite they are built from.
```

